Write a JQF property-based test to reproduce this bug.

Project root: /home/byoungwoo/preserve-testing/experiments/main/workspace/Time-4/source
Test file: src/test/java/org/joda/time/JQF_TestPartial_Basics.java
Test class: org.joda.time.JQF_TestPartial_Basics
Test method: testBugReproduction

Bug summary: Partial.with(...) doesn't validate duplicate-duration/overlapping field types (e.g. clockhourOfDay and hourOfDay), so it creates an invalid Partial, while the Partial(DateTimeFieldType[], int[]) constructor rejects the same combination with "Types array must not contain duplicate". The invalid Partial then causes isEqual to throw "objects must have matching field types".

Concrete examples:
[{'input': 'new Partial(new DateTimeFieldType[] { clockhourOfDay(), hourOfDay() }, new int[] { 1, 1 })', 'expected': 'Throws IllegalArgumentException (Types array must not contain duplicate)', 'actual': 'Throws IllegalArgumentException: Types array must not contain duplicate (constructor is consistent/correct)'}, {'input': 'new Partial(clockhourOfDay(), 1).with(hourOfDay(), 1)', 'expected': 'Throws IllegalArgumentException, consistent with the array constructor, since the fields are duplicates', 'actual': 'Returns an invalid Partial: [clockhourOfDay=1, hourOfDay=1]'}, {'input': 'new Partial(clockhourOfDay(), 1).with(hourOfDay(), 1).isEqual(new Partial(hourOfDay(), 1).with(clockhourOfDay(), 1))', 'expected': 'Not reached; the with() call should throw earlier because the Partial is invalid', 'actual': 'Throws IllegalArgumentException: objects must have matching field types'}]

